\caption(Visible three-epoch Adam updates every learned leaf in both streams. Official validation NLL selects the restored checkpoint;independent immutable RGB-only deployment is checked before the held-out comparison.)
\alttext(Visible three-epoch Adam updates every learned leaf in both streams. Official validation NLL selects the restored checkpoint;independent immutable RGB-only deployment is checked before the held-out comparison.)

\image(figures/bilinear_cnn_confusion.svg)
\caption(Restored six-class model confusion on48 official test images,eight per true class. True classes increase bottom to top,predicted classes left to right;the common count range is[0,8].)
\alttext(Actual held-out six-class flower confusion counts.)

\image(figures/bilinear_cnn_predictions.png)
\caption(All48 actual held-out flower photographs in source order,eight per local class. Green/red borders mark correct/wrong predictions. Six probability bars sit below each photograph;the true-class bar is green and the other class bars blue.)
\alttext(All actual held-out flower images with prediction borders and six-class probability bars.)

This from-scratch three-epoch/six-class subset is small and deliberately
bounded. Its held-out results and visible error images describe this prepared
protocol. They do not establish full102-class fine-grained performance or
paper accuracy. Compare the printed color and untrained references before
attributing success to learned local feature interactions.

The restored model selects epoch3 and correctly classifies16/48 held-out
images(33.33%),versus18/48(37.5%) for the fitting-only color reference and
8/48(16.67%) for its untrained initialization. Its NLL1.780632 is lower than
the color reference2.289061,so accuracy and probability quality tell different
stories. These36 updates do not establish an accuracy advantage over mean RGB;
the all-image gallery makes the remaining errors visible.

> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

\image(figures/bilinear_cnn_confusion_api_0.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 1. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)


> Conclusion

The bilinear interaction differs from multiplying separate averages,and its two gradient paths are independently checked. Actual photographs,explicit data preparation,visible SGD/Adam and immutable deployment make the bounded experiment reproducible. The held-out images and simple references expose how much this small from-scratch model has actually learned.

> References

- [Lin,RoyChowdhury and Maji(2015),Bilinear CNNs for Fine-grained Visual Recognition](https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.07889v3).
- [Nilsback and Zisserman(2008),Oxford102 Flower Dataset and official splits](https://www.robots.ox.ac.uk/~vgg/data/flowers/102/).
- [Torchvision Flowers102 dataset source,published archive checksums and class names](https://github.com/pytorch/vision/blob/main/torchvision/datasets/flowers102.py).
